{\centering\fontsize{16}{20}\selectfont\textbf{Paper 1: Comparative Analysis of CPU Scheduling Algorithms for Performance Efficiency}\par}
\vspace{1em}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Problem:} The paper evaluates the efficiency of various CPU scheduling algorithms to determine how the choice of strategy influences system responsiveness, throughput, waiting time, and turnaround time.

    \item \textbf{Method:} The author set up a uniprocessor simulation framework to calculate and compare performance metrics using Gantt charts for FCFS, SJF, SRTF, RR, Priority, HRRN, and FKS algorithms.
    
    \item \textbf{Data / Experimental Setting:} The experiment used a single dataset featuring five processes (P1-P5) with predetermined burst times and priorities.
    
    \item \textbf{Relevant Finding:} The Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF) algorithm achieved the lowest average turnaround time (34.2 ms) and waiting time (19.2 ms), while Round Robin recorded the highest average turnaround time (51 ms) and waiting time (36 ms). 
    
    \item \textbf{Evidence:} The findings are supported by Table 3 (Average Turnaround Time and Average Waiting Time) and execution snapshot Gantt charts (Figures 1 and 2).
    
    \item \textbf{Conditions/Assumptions:} The findings hold under the strict assumptions that all jobs are ready at time zero, burst times are known in advance, processes run to completion without I/O interruptions, and context-switching time is negligible.
    
    \item \textbf{Limitations:} The algorithms that performed best (SRTF and FKS) rely on the unrealistic assumption of knowing exact future burst times. Additionally, the study's assumption of zero context-switching overhead ignores the primary drawback of preemptive algorithms.
    
    \item \textbf{Contribution to Our Question:} For our focused question, this paper tells us that preemptive algorithms like SRTF minimize waiting times for predictable workloads, though they rely on unrealistic assumptions. It also shows that while Round Robin is fair for interactive systems, it can severely degrade waiting time and throughput if the time quantum is sized incorrectly.


\end{itemize}




