{\centering\fontsize{16}{20}\selectfont\textbf{Paper 2: Comparative Analysis of Different CPU Scheduling Algorithms with Respect to Average Waiting Time and Average Turnaround Time}\par}
\vspace{1em}


\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Problem:} The paper analyzes the relationship between existing CPU scheduling algorithms (FCFS, SJF, RR, and Priority Based) with respect to their average waiting time and turnaround time to identify the fastest algorithm.

    \item \textbf{Method:} The authors designed five mathematical cases (altering CPU burst times by adding or subtracting specific values) and calculated the Average Waiting Time (AWT) and Average Turnaround Time (ATAT) for each algorithm.
    
    \item \textbf{Data / Experimental Setting:} A ready queue consisting of four processes (P1, P2, P3, P4) with varying CPU burst times and priorities tested across 5 rigid mathematical cases.
    
    \item \textbf{Relevant Finding:} The SJF algorithm consistently produced the lowest average waiting time and turnaround time across all five test cases. Furthermore, CPU burst time was found to be directly proportional to average waiting and turnaround times.
    
    \item \textbf{Evidence:} The findings are supported by calculated data in Tables 8 through 11 and visualized using 3-D line graphs (Figures 1-4) for each algorithm.
    
    \item \textbf{Conditions/Assumptions:}  The findings strictly apply to the five formulated cases where CPU burst times scale up and down linearly (e.g., adding 1, subtracting 2).
    
    \item \textbf{Limitations:} The study only evaluates waiting time and turnaround time, completely ignoring other critical metrics like response time, throughput, or fairness.
    
    \item \textbf{Contribution to Our Question:} For our focused question, this paper tells us that SJF provides the absolute lowest waiting time regardless of how the burst times scale mathematically. However, because it ignores throughput and fairness, it cannot explain how these workloads behave in interactive, real-world environments.


\end{itemize}




