\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Which dimensions can reasonably be compared across all three (in your case, both) papers?} \\
     We can directly compare the methodologies and the evaluation metrics. Both papers evaluate CPU scheduling algorithms—specifically FCFS, SJF, and Round Robin—by calculating the Average Waiting Time (AWT) and Average Turnaround Time (ATAT).

    \item \textbf{Where do the papers appear to provide similar evidence?} \\
    Both papers provide identical conclusions regarding the Shortest Job First (SJF) approach minimizing wait times. Paper 1 found that SRTF (the preemptive version of SJF) yielded the lowest waiting time and turnaround time for its dataset. Similarly, Paper 2 verified that SJF consistently provided the lowest average waiting time across all 5 of its distinct experimental cases.
    
    \item \textbf{Where do you notice an apparent difference?} \\
    The experimental setups and methods of analysis differ significantly. Paper 1 used a single, static dataset of 5 processes to evaluate 7 different algorithms. In contrast, Paper 2 used a baseline of 4 processes but actively modified their CPU burst times mathematically (e.g., adding 1, subtracting 2) across 5 different cases to observe how the waiting time scaled.
    
    \item \textbf{Is there any important information needed for answering your question that one or more papers do not provide?} \\
    Yes. Our group's focused research question explicitly asks about "response time," "throughput," and "fairness." However, both papers focused almost exclusively on waiting time and turnaround time metrics, failing to provide hard quantitative data, charts, or dedicated experiments for measuring fairness (starvation rates) or interactive response times.
    
\end{itemize}

